{#-
  /boot/firmware/cmdline.txt - the kernel command line. ONE line, space
  separated. Managed by Ansible role: rpi_nvme. Do not edit on the Pi.

  Every token already on the line is preserved in its original order, except
  tokens whose key is one we manage - those are dropped and re-added from
  rpi_nvme.cmdline.params. That is what makes this idempotent: running it
  twice cannot duplicate a parameter or leave two conflicting values.

  A malformed line here does not degrade the Pi, it stops it booting, and
  this host has no console. The task that renders this validates that root=
  survived before replacing the file, and keeps a backup.
-#}
{#-
  Disabling the host memory buffer is escalation step 3 and off by default, so
  it is appended here rather than sitting in params as a token that is usually
  wrong. Its key then becomes managed, which is what drops the firmware's own
  `nvme.max_host_mem_size_mb=32`. While it stays off the key is unmanaged and
  the firmware value is preserved untouched. See rpi_nvme.hmb in defaults.
-#}
{%- set hmb = ['nvme.max_host_mem_size_mb=0'] if (rpi_nvme.hmb.disable | default(false) | bool) else [] -%}
{%- set params = rpi_nvme.cmdline.params + hmb -%}
{%- set managed_keys = params | map('regex_replace', '=.*$', '') | list -%}
{%- set managed_re = '^(' ~ (managed_keys | map('regex_escape') | join('|')) ~ ')=' -%}
{%- set kept = rpi_nvme_cmdline_tokens.split() | reject('match', managed_re) | list -%}
{{ (kept + params) | join(' ') }}
